      "content": "# Transaction Isolation, Race Conditions, and Deadlock Prevention\n\n## 1. Transaction Isolation\n\nTransaction isolation defines how and when the changes made by one transaction become visible to other concurrent transactions. The SQL standard defines four isolation levels, each offering a different balance between **consistency** and **concurrency**.\n\n### The Four Isolation Levels\n\n| Isolation Level | Dirty Read | Non-Repeatable Read | Phantom Read |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Read Uncommitted** | Possible | Possible | Possible |\n| **Read Committed** | Prevented | Possible | Possible |\n| **Repeatable Read** | Prevented | Prevented | Possible* |\n| **Serializable** | Prevented | Prevented | Prevented |\n\n*\\*Some databases (e.g., PostgreSQL) prevent phantom reads at Repeatable Read via MVCC.*\n\n### Example: Bank Transfer\n\nConsider two accounts, **A** (balance $100) and **B** (balance $50). Two transactions run concurrently:\n\n- **T1**: Transfer $10 from A to B.\n- **T2**: Read the total balance of A and B.\n\n#### At `READ UNCOMMITTED`:",
